      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional haulier in West Yorkshire. First you're on the red team: look at what's public about Tarrowby, then write one message aimed at Tarrowby staff on their phones, choosing the story and whether it arrives as a text or as a printed notice with a QR code. Then you swap seats and go through what's waiting on a Tarrowby work phone. Only the phone is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Now it lands on you",
        "flipIntro": "This is the message you just wrote, as a Tarrowby driver would see it on their phone. The parts picked out below are what make it believable.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "Bogdan read it in his cab at the Halifax drop, between unloading and his next slot. On a small screen it looked like something Tarrowby would send, so he tapped through. People who fall for messages like this are not stupid: this one was built to look right.",
        "flipNoSend": "Your message didn't go out this time, so there's nothing of yours to show. Next, the same kind of message arrives on a Tarrowby work phone, and you're the one reading it.",
        "noBlameLine": "Nobody is marked down for a miss here. Every one of these was built to look right, and the people who tap on them are reading a small screen between jobs, not being careless.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: if a text, a notice or an email wants you to scan a code or tap a short link to pay, sign in or confirm something, stop. Don't reply, not even STOP. Open the app or site the way you normally would, or ring the sender on a number you already hold, then report the message the way your organisation asks you to.",
        "defendOnly": "You went straight to the phone this time. These messages were written by people whose job is to make them look right, and people who fall for messages like these are not stupid. Each card shows what gave the fake ones away, and why the real ones were fine.",
        "defendIntro": "Go through the work phone and decide what to do with each text, notice and email. On a notice, check where the code goes before you would scan it. Take your time: there is no trick to it, just the habits that catch these."
      },
